1. Introduction 
Fiber-reinforced polymer (FRP) composites are well known for their favorable properties such as low weight, high stiffness, high specific strength, low cost, corrosion resistance, and being eco-friendly [1] , [2] . Also, one of their outstanding characteristics is the inherent anisotropy which gives the designer a facility to tailor the composites to realize the anticipated requirements. Due to these characteristics, laminated FRP composite materials are broadly applied in automotive, aerospace, sports, coating, pipelines, and electronics industries [3] , [4] . Polymer resin and fiber reinforcement types, and fiber lay-up and fabrication methods are chosen and evaluated according to the desired engineering applications [5] . 
Carbon fiber-reinforced epoxy composites (CFRE) are gaining popularity as high-performance synthetic materials manufactured from the combination of two, or more, different phases of materials [6] . They are superior materials with high stiffness, strength properties workable up to 2000 °C (3632 °F), low density, and long fatigue life over many other conventional composites [7] , [8] . Thanks to their excellent mechanical properties, they were broadly introduced in a variety of structural applications such as aerospace, automotive, and construction industries. The two main essential phases in developing CFRE composites are polymer matrix and fiber reinforcement [9] . The combination of these phases results in versatile mechanical properties that cannot be accomplished with a single material. Carbon fibers (CF) have 93 to 95 % carbon content and are produced at about 1300 °C (2372 °F). Epoxy resin, on the other hand, has been of substantial significance to engineering for many decades. Furthermore, composite materials made of fiber/epoxy combination have provided outstanding thermal, mechanical, and electrical properties [10] . 
Generally, the design of FRP structures needs knowledge of the stresses and strains in the composite individuals as well as in the bulk lamina. Experimental studies including tension, bending, torsion, and impact tests are frequently introduced to determine the mechanical properties of polymer composites [6] , [11] . This data could be, also, theoretically obtained by the mechanical analysis of the lamina, or by modeling methods [12] . 
The global characteristics of FRP composite materials are affected by many factors [10] . The orientation of the fiber is considered a vital parameter that affects the mechanical response of composites [13] . Therefore, many previous and current researches are aimed to examine the influences of fiber orientation on the mechanical characteristics of FRP composites [14] , [15] , [16] . Banakar et al. [17] experimentally investigated the role of fiber orientation on the mechanical properties of CFRE. The composite laminates were fabricated from bi-woven weft-type carbon fiber and epoxy resin. The tensile resistance of the composites was explored at ± 30°, ±45 o , and ± 90° orientation angles. The strength properties were superior in the case of 90° fiber orientation because the applied tensile load was distributed, in an equal manner, on all the fibers and transmitted along the axis of the fibers. However, Keshavamurthy et al. [18] observed that the maximum Yield strength, stiffness, and load-carrying capacity realized at the 0° fiber orientation in ply laminated glass fiber reinforced epoxy GFRE composites. Bassey et al. [19] introduced an experimental and analytical study of the consequence of fiber orientation and fiber content on the tensile strength of GFRE composites using the Taguchi experimental technique. They suggested that 45° fiber orientation and 50 % fiber content resulted in the optimum tensile strength of the tested composite. Patl et al. [9] explored the impact of fiber orientation on the tensile strength of thin CFRE composites fabricated using bi-directional woven reinforcements. Through the Bi-directional carbon fiber three fiber orientations were selected for testing: [0°/90°], [30°/60°], and [45°/−45 o ]. The results obtained from tensile tests established that the highest tensile strength was obtained at 0°/90° fiber orientation in which flat failure and fiber delamination were detected. In the weft direction, fibers remained undamaged, while in the warp direction, fiber pullout and fiber breakage were found. It is also inferred that the specimens with 30° and 45° fiber orientation had lower tensile strength than those with a 0° fiber orientation. They postulated that the tensile force acting on fiber laminate divided into warp and weft directions. 
Finding comprehensive literature that consider the effect of orientation in the case of unidirectional fiber laminated composites is still difficult. In fact, to the best of our knowledge, only a few studies considered such an aspect. Rahmani et al. [20] documented the influence of fiber orientation on the mechanical properties of unidirectional CFRE laminated composites. The tensile properties in terms of modulus, strength, and elongation at break were examined following ASTM D-638 (2014) [21] , [22] standard method using a universal testing machine (STM-150). The effect of fiber orientation was determined at angles of 0°, 35°, 45°, and 90°. The composites with 0°, 35°, 0 o, and 0°, –35°, 0°, +35°, and 0° orientations showed the highest tensile strengths and moduli among the tested composites. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) examination of tested specimens suggested that the failure mechanism was attributed to fiber fracture due to tensile force. The micrographs showed that there is no evidence of fiber pull-out, matrix cracking, interface debonding, or interlaminar delamination. As also recently pointed out by Parmiggiani et al. [23] , the influence of the fiber orientation on the tensile properties of unidirectional CF/Nylon composites was significantly justified if the fibers were aligned with the direction of the applied tensile load (i.e., at 0° orientation). Additionally, the variation of both elastic modulus and tensile strength was not linear with the angle between fibers' direction and the tensile load. A quick deterioration in mechanical properties was notably detected when increasing the fiber angle to + 45°. The related failure modes and stress–strain curve of the 0° orientation specimen suggested high strength and stiffness, along with little deformation capability. Consequently, sudden brittle rupture of the specimens was expected as soon as breakage of the CF occurred. 
Concerning the above-mentioned studies, the tensile characteristics of unidirectional CF-reinforced polymer composites are highly affected by fiber orientation. However, there is no unique agreement about the most critical orientation. Thus, there is a massive need for more research to enrich comprehensive knowledge in such cases. The present work aims to identify the influence of fiber orientation on the mechanical properties of CFRE when subjected to tensile load. The theoretical data determined from the strength failure theories are compared with those obtained experimentally from simple tension tests. 
